  <subsection xml:id="drop-lowest-midterm">
    <title>Dropping the Lowest Midterm Grade</title>

    <p>
      Your lowest midterm exam score is dropped. Your best two midterms, out
      of <m>40</m> points, are scaled to the full <m>60</m> midterm points, so
      the midterms still count for <m>60\%</m> of your course grade:
      <md>
        \text{Midterm points} = \frac{\text{sum of your best two midterms}}{40}\times 60.
      </md>
      The final exam (<m>30</m> points) and the assignments (<m>10</m> points)
      are unchanged, and your total out of <m>100</m> becomes a letter grade
      with the percentage ranges in <xref ref="table-letter-grades"/>.
    </p>

    <table xml:id="table-grade-drop-example">
      <title>An example: from a C+ to a B+</title>
      <tabular halign="left" top="minor" bottom="minor">
        <col/>
        <col halign="right"/>
        <col halign="right"/>
        <col halign="right"/>
        <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
          <cell>Component</cell>
          <cell>Score</cell>
          <cell>Without the drop</cell>
          <cell>With the drop</cell>
        </row>
        <row><cell>Exam #1</cell><cell><m>8/20</m></cell><cell><m>8</m></cell><cell>dropped</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Exam #2</cell><cell><m>18/20</m></cell><cell><m>18</m></cell><cell><m>18</m></cell></row>
        <row><cell>Exam #3</cell><cell><m>18/20</m></cell><cell><m>18</m></cell><cell><m>18</m></cell></row>
        <row>
          <cell>Midterm points (out of 60)</cell>
          <cell/>
          <cell><m>8 + 18 + 18 = 44</m></cell>
          <cell><m>\frac{18 + 18}{40}\times 60 = 54</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row><cell>Final Exam</cell><cell><m>25/30</m></cell><cell><m>25</m></cell><cell><m>25</m></cell></row>
        <row bottom="minor"><cell>Assignments</cell><cell><m>9/10</m></cell><cell><m>9</m></cell><cell><m>9</m></cell></row>
        <row><cell>Total (out of 100)</cell><cell/><cell><m>78</m> (C+)</cell><cell><m>88</m> (B+)</cell></row>
      </tabular>
    </table>

    <p>
      The best two midterms come to <m>36</m> out of <m>40</m>, which is
      <m>90\%</m>, and that <m>90\%</m> counts for all <m>60</m> midterm
      points. The weak first exam simply stops counting.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="figure-grade-calculator">
      <caption>Enter your scores to see your course grade with the lowest
      midterm dropped. Leave a box empty for an exam you have not taken yet:
      empty boxes count as <m>0</m>, so the total shown is the lowest the
      scores you entered can give, and your letter grade appears once all
      five scores are entered. While the final is still to come, the
      calculator lists the final exam score each letter grade needs. (In the
      PDF, scan the code to open the calculator on the course website.)</caption>
      <interactive xml:id="interactive-grade-calculator"
                   iframe="grade-calculator.html"
                   preview="grade-calculator-preview.png"
                   width="100%" aspect="15:16"/>
    </figure>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="letter-grades">
    <title>Letter Grades</title>

    <figure xml:id="fig-grading">
      <caption>Your course grade comes from three exams, the final exam, and
      ten written assignments.</caption>
      <image source="grading.png" width="84%">
        <shortdescription>An instructor at a desk grading papers, holding up one marked with a circled A</shortdescription>
        <description>
          <p>
            A cartoon of an instructor sitting at a desk, smiling and holding
            up a graded paper with a large letter A circled in red. A tall
            stack of assignments with colored sticky-note tabs sits on the left
            of the desk, and two more papers marked with a circled A lie in
            front of him. He has a red pen in his other hand, and there is a
            mug of pens, a stack of books, and a potted plant on the desk.
          </p>
        </description>
      </image>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Your total out of 100 points is converted to a letter grade using the
      percentage ranges in <xref ref="table-letter-grades"/>.
    </p>

    <table xml:id="table-letter-grades">
      <title>Percentage ranges and letter grades</title>
      <tabular halign="left" top="minor" bottom="minor">
        <col width="50%"/>
        <col width="50%"/>
        <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
          <cell>Letter grade</cell>
          <cell>Percentage range</cell>
        </row>
        <row><cell>A</cell><cell>94 <ndash/> 100</cell></row>
        <row><cell>A<ndash/></cell><cell>90 <ndash/> 93</cell></row>
        <row><cell>B+</cell><cell>87 <ndash/> 89</cell></row>
        <row><cell>B</cell><cell>83 <ndash/> 86</cell></row>
        <row><cell>B<ndash/></cell><cell>80 <ndash/> 82</cell></row>
        <row><cell>C+</cell><cell>77 <ndash/> 79</cell></row>
        <row><cell>C</cell><cell>73 <ndash/> 76</cell></row>
        <row><cell>C<ndash/></cell><cell>70 <ndash/> 72</cell></row>
        <row><cell>D+</cell><cell>67 <ndash/> 69</cell></row>
        <row><cell>D</cell><cell>63 <ndash/> 66</cell></row>
        <row><cell>D<ndash/></cell><cell>60 <ndash/> 62</cell></row>
        <row><cell>F</cell><cell>59 or lower</cell></row>
      </tabular>
    </table>
  </subsection>
</section>
